# Atlassian Provider

Use the Atlassian provider to manage Atlassian Cloud resources. The provider
covers more than one Atlassian API family, and each family has its own
credential. Configure only the families you use; a type whose family is not
configured reports exactly which attribute to set.

## Cloud Admin APIs

Organization and Admin Control types authenticate with an Atlassian
organization API key. Supply it as `admin_api_key` or, preferably, through the
`ATLASSIAN_ADMIN_API_KEY` environment variable.

## Confluence Cloud

Confluence types authenticate against a site in one of three ways: as a service
account with OAuth 2.0 client credentials, as a service account with an API
token issued to it, or as an Atlassian account over HTTP basic auth. Configure
exactly one.

**Service account with client credentials (recommended for automation).** An
OAuth 2.0 credential created for a service account in the admin console.
Requests go through the `api.atlassian.com` gateway, so the site is identified
by its cloud id, which the provider discovers from `site_url` when `cloud_id`
is omitted.

```hcl
provider "atlassian" {
  site_url = "https://example.atlassian.net"

  service_account = {
    client_id     = var.client_id      # or ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_CLIENT_ID
    client_secret = var.client_secret  # or ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_CLIENT_SECRET
  }
}
```

The credential's scopes bound what the provider can do, and **a credential
holds at most 50**. Each data source and resource page lists the scopes that
type needs; grant the union of the ones your configuration uses. A scope is
checked when an operation runs, so a type you only read from costs only its
read scope.

When one credential cannot hold every scope you need, configure several
provider aliases, each with its own credential, and issue all of them from the
**same service account**: the account that creates or updates a space is
recorded on it, so credentials from different accounts would leave objects
attributed to more than one identity.

```hcl
provider "atlassian" {
  alias    = "spaces_readonly"
  site_url = "https://example.atlassian.net"
  service_account = {
    client_id     = var.readonly_client_id
    client_secret = var.readonly_client_secret
  }
}

provider "atlassian" {
  alias    = "spaces_admin"
  site_url = "https://example.atlassian.net"
  service_account = {
    client_id     = var.admin_client_id
    client_secret = var.admin_client_secret
  }
}

data "atlassian_confluence_spaces" "all" {
  provider = atlassian.spaces_readonly
}

resource "atlassian_confluence_space" "docs" {
  provider = atlassian.spaces_admin
  key      = "DOCS"
  name     = "Documentation"
}
```

**Service account with an API token.** An API token issued to the same kind of
service account, sent as a bearer token. It also goes through the
`api.atlassian.com` gateway and identifies the site the same way, but there is
no token exchange: the token is used as configured.

```hcl
provider "atlassian" {
  site_url = "https://example.atlassian.net"

  service_account = {
    api_token = var.service_account_api_token # or ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_API_TOKEN
  }
}
```

Its scopes are chosen when the token is created and **cannot be changed
afterwards**, so issue it with every scope the configuration needs. One detail
does not apply to client credentials: the Confluence operations this provider
runs against the v1 REST API are reachable by an API token only when it also
carries the classic scope names, so a token that manages a space through its
whole lifecycle needs `write:confluence-space` and
`read:confluence-space.summary` alongside the granular ones each resource page
lists. Every `service_account` attribute falls back to a variable under one prefix:
`ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_CLIENT_ID`,
`ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_CLIENT_SECRET`,
`ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_API_TOKEN` and
`ATLASSIAN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_CLOUD_ID`. `ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN` is basic auth's
variable and does not configure this credential.

**Atlassian account with an API token.** HTTP basic auth as a user, with an API
token created at https://id.atlassian.com/manage/api-tokens. Authorization
follows that user's own permissions.

```hcl
provider "atlassian" {
  site_url = "https://example.atlassian.net"

  basic_auth = {
    email     = var.email      # or ATLASSIAN_EMAIL
    api_token = var.api_token  # or ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN
  }
}
```

Every credential attribute falls back to an environment variable, so a block
may be omitted entirely when its variables are set. Because the variable
names are shared with other Atlassian tooling, a configuration error about
conflicting credentials states where each value came from.

